\uc1 \u160 ?}{\rtlch \fcs1 \af5 \afs24 \ltrch \fcs0 \fs24 \rtlch \alang1025 \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af5 \loch \f5 \lang1033 \langnp1033 \langfe2052 \langfenp2052 \alang1025 \par }\pard \plain \li0 \ri0 \widctlpar \aspalpha \aspnum \adjustright \lin0 \rin0 \itap0 \rtlch \fcs1 \af0 \afs24 \ltrch \fcs0 \fs24 \rtlch \alang1025 \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af4 \loch \af4 \lang1033 \langnp1033 \langfe2052 \langfenp2052 \alang1025 {\rtlch \fcs1 \af5 \afs20 \ltrch \fcs0 \fs20 \rtlch \alang1025 \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af5 \loch \f5 \lang1033 \langnp1033 \langfe2052 \langfenp2052 \alang1025 INVOCATION TO THE MUSE.\uc1 \u8212 ?Chrys\uc1 \u234 ?s, priest of Apollo, comes to the Achaian camp in hope to redeem his daughter.\uc1 \u160 ?Agamemnon, however, the commander in chief, whose prize she is, spurns his petition.\uc1 \u160 ?\uc1 \u160 ?The old priest prays his god to avenge him: Apollo sends a fatal pestilence into the Achaian camp.\uc1 \u160 ?\uc1 \u160 ?Achill\uc1 \u234 ?s calls an assembly for enquiry; at which the augur Calchas declares that Agamemnon is the cause of the plauge: Agamemnon agrees to give up the damsel, but demands an equivalent to be given him.\uc1 \u160 ?\uc1 \u160 ?Achill\uc1 \u234 ?s accuses him of avarice: Agamemnon declares he will take an equivalent by force.\uc1 \u160 ?\uc1 \u160 ?Achill\uc1 \u234 ?s rates him roundly, and threatens to quit and go home.\uc1 \u160 ?\uc1 \u160 ?Agamemnon in return threatens to take from him his prize damsel, Bris\uc1 \u234 ?is.\uc1 \u8212 ?Achill\uc1 \u234 ?s is on the point of drawing his sword on Agamemnon, but Ath\uc1 \u234 ?n\uc1 \u232 ?, the goddess of Wisdom, checks him: still they wrangle, till the assembly is broken up.\uc1 \u160 ?\uc1 \u160 ?Agamemnon takes Bris\uc1 \u234 ?is, as he had threatened.\uc1 \u160 ?\uc1 \u160 ?Achill\uc1 \u234 ?s invokes his goddess-mother, Thetis, to ask Zeus for vengeance on Agamemnon.\uc1 \u160 ?\uc1 \u160 ?This she does, and obtains his assent.\uc1 \u160 ?\uc1 \u160 ?Thence aris