see a century or so in which nobody will try again the put the\uc1 \u160 ?}{\rtlch \fcs1 \af0 \afs24 \ltrch \fcs0 \i1 \fs24 \rtlch \alang1025 \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af5 \loch \f5 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe2052 \langfenp2052 \alang1025 Iliad}{\rtlch \fcs1 \af0 \afs24 \ltrch \fcs0 \fs24 \rtlch \alang1025 \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af5 \loch \f5 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe2052 \langfenp2052 \alang1025 \uc1 \u160 ?into English verse.\uc1 \u160 ?Taste may change greatly, but it looks to me as if Mr. Lattimore\uc1 \u8217 ?s version would survive at least as long as Pope\uc1 \u8217 ?s, for in its way it is quite as solidly distinguished,\uc1 \u8221 ? a remark, ironically enough, from Robert Fitzgerald, whose translation of the\uc1 \u160 ?Iliad (which many people, myself included, believe superior to Lattimore\uc1 \u8217 ?s) appeared in 1963.\uc1 \u160 ?\uc1 \u160 ?And since its appearance, Lattimore\uc1 \u8217 ?s\uc1 \u160 ?Iliad\uc1 \u160 ?has remained very popular (in a survey conducted in 1987, Lattimore\uc1 \u8217 ?s translation was preferred by three quarters of the respondents). }{\rtlch \fcs1 \af0 \afs24 \ltrch \fcs0 \fs24 \rtlch \alang1025 \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af5 \loch \f5 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe2052 \langfenp2052 \alang1025 \par }\pard \plain \li0 \ri0 \widctlpar \aspalpha \aspnum \adjustright \lin0 \rin0 \itap0 \rtlch \fcs1 \af0 \afs24 \ltrch \fcs0 \fs24 \rtlch \alang1025 \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af4 \loch \af4 \lang1033 \langnp1033 \langfe2052 \langfenp2052 \alang1025 {\rtlch \fcs1 \af0 \afs24 \ltrch \fcs0 \fs24 \rtlch \alang1025 \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af5 \loch \f5 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe2052 \langfenp2052 \alang1025 \uc1 \u160 ?}{\rtlch \fcs1 \af0 \afs24 \ltrch \fcs0 \fs24 \rtlch \alang1025 \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af5 \loch \f5 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe2052 \langfenp2052 \alang1025 \par }\pard \plain \li0 \ri0 \widctlpar \aspalpha \aspnum \adjustright \lin0 \rin0 \itap0 \rtlch \fcs1 \af0 \afs24 \ltrch \fcs0 \fs24 \rtlch \alang1025 \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af4 \loch \af4 \lang1033 \langnp1033 \langfe2052 \langfenp2052 \alang1025 {\rtlch \fcs1 \af0 \afs24 \ltrch \fcs0 \fs24 \rtlch \alang1025 \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af5 \loch \f5 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe2052 \langfenp2052 \alang1025 \uc1 \u160 ?Lattimore\uc1 \u8217 ?s translation uses a six-beat line with a flexible number of syllables and strives for a line-by-line fidelity to the Greek text, a habit which the first person to try it (T. S. Brandeth in 1846) described as having \uc1 \u8220 ?no great merit,\uc1 \u8221 ? an opinion with which I concur wholeheartedly.\uc1 \u160 ?\uc1 \u160 ?Even the advantage of being able to reference the Greek text easily is unavailable in Lattimore\uc1 \u8217 ?s text because his version does not number the lines.\uc1 \u160 ?\uc1 \u160 ?His vocabulary is, for the most part, as he says, \uc1 \u8220 ?my own \uc1 \u8216 ?poetical language,\uc1 \u8217 ? which is mostly the plain English of today.\uc1 \u8221 ?\uc1 \u160 ?\uc1 \u160 ?The result earned praise for rescuing Homer from prose translations, which had outnumbered poetical renditions in the previous years.\uc1 \u160 ?\uc1 \u160 ?And many readers obviously like the result\uc1 \u8212 ?a \uc1 \u8220 ?weighty\uc1 \u8221 ? poem which keeps them moving through the text and living up to Lattimore\uc1 \u8217 ?s eminently pragmatic answer to Arnold: \uc1 \u8220 ?I do not think\uc1 \u160 ?nobility\uc1 \u160 ?is a quality to be directly striven for; you must write as well as you can, and then see, or let others see, whether or not the result is noble.\uc1 \u8221 ?\uc1 \u160 ?\uc1 \u160 ?Lattimore may also have put the rest the endless debates about the suitability of the hexameter for English verse (although, in fairness to the other debaters, one should note that his hexameter is much more flexible than they were prepared to admit). }{\rtlch \fcs1 \af0 \afs24 \ltrch \fcs0 \fs24 \rtlch \alang1025 \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af5 \loch \f5 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe2052 \langfenp2052 \alang1025 \par }\pard \plain \li0 \ri0 \widctlpar \aspalpha \aspnum \adjustright \lin0 \rin0 \itap0 \rtlch \fcs1 \af0 \afs24 \ltrch \fcs0 \fs24 \rtlch \alang1025 \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af4 \loch \af4 \lang1033 \langnp1033 \langfe2052 \langfenp2052 \alang1025 {\rtlch \fcs1 \af0 \afs24 \ltrch \fcs0 \fs24 \rtlch \alang1025 \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af5 \loch \f5 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe2052 \langfenp2052 \alang1025 \uc1 \u160 ?}{\rtlch \fcs1 \af0 \afs24 \ltrch \fcs0 \fs24 \rtlch \alang1025 \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af5 \loch \f5 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe2052 \langfenp2052 \alang1025 \par }\pard \plain \li0 \ri0 \widctlpar \aspalpha \aspnum \adjustright \lin0 \rin0 \itap0 \rtlch \fcs1 \af0 \afs24 \ltrch \fcs0 \fs24 \rtlch \alang1025 \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af4 \loch \af4 \lang1033 \langnp1033 \langfe2052 \langfenp2052 \alang1025 {\rtlch \fcs1 \af0 \afs24 \ltrch \fcs0 \fs24 \rtlch \alang1025 \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af5 \loch \f5 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe2052 \langfenp2052 \alang1025 Lattimore has also had his critics who complain about various things, including his syntax (in Knopff\uc1 \u8217 ?s words: \uc1 \u8220 ?misprints, mistranslations, obscurities, or outrages to the English language\uc1 \u8221 ?).\uc1 \u160 ?The plainness in the vocabulary is not matched by the clarity in the sentences, so that (as in many of Lattimore\uc1 \u8217 ?s other translations\uc1 \u8212 ?of Aeschylus, for example) there is a constant need to pause in order to sort out just what a particular phrase or\uc1 \u160 ?\uc1 \u160 ?sentence means (\uc1 \u8220 ?I, who am such as no other of the bronze-armoured Achaians,\uc1 \u8221 ? \uc1 \u8220 ?My mother bore me not utterly lacking in warcraft,\uc1 \u8221 ? and so on).\uc1 \u160 ?\uc1 \u160 ?Here\uc1 \u8217 ?s a random sample, taken from a moment in Achilles\uc1 \u8217 ? response to Odysseus: \uc1 \u8220 ?not if he gave me gifts as many as the sand or the dust is.\uc1 \u8221 ?\uc1 \u160 ?\uc1 \u160 ?Clear enough perhaps, but not idiomatic English, for sand cannot be \uc1 \u8220 ?many\uc1 \u8221 ? any more than \uc1 \u8220 ?dust\uc1 \u8221 ? can.\uc1 \u160 ?\uc1 \u160 ?We have to supply the missing: \uc1 \u8220 ?grains of . . .\uc1 \u8221 ?\uc1 \u160 ?\uc1 \u160 ?A small example but not untypical\uc1 \u8212 ?and, in my view, very irritating.\uc1 \u160 ?\uc1 \u160 ?Here, too, the rhythm maintains the hexameter but in the process ends up sounding awkward and forced, anything but an outburst from a passionate man who has worked himself up into a temper.\uc1 \u160 ?\uc1 \u160 ?Reading Lattimore\uc1 \u8217 ?s\uc1 \u160 ?}{\rtlch \fcs1 \af0 \afs24 \ltrch \fcs0 \i1 \fs24 \rtlch \alang1025 \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af5 \loch \f5 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe2052 \langfenp2052 \alang1025 Iliad}{\rtlch \fcs1 \af0 \afs24 \ltrch \fcs0 \fs24 \rtlch \alang1025 \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af5 \loch \f5 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe2052 \langfenp2052 \alang1025 \uc1 \u160 ?I\uc1 \u8217 ?m always reminded of Dr. Leavis\uc1 \u8217 ? comment about how Milton\uc1 \u8217 ?s verse \uc1 \u8220 ?calls pervasively for a kind of attention \uc1 \u8230 ? toward itself.\uc1 \u8221 ? }{\rtlch \fcs1 \af0 \afs24 \ltrch \fcs0 \fs24 \rtlch \alang1025 \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af5 \loch \f5 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe2052 \langfenp2052 \alang1025 \par }\pard \plain \li0 \ri0 \widctlpar \aspalpha \aspnum \adjustright \lin0 \rin0 \itap0 \rtlch \fcs1 \af0 \afs24 \ltrch \fcs0 \fs24 \rtlch \alang1025 \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af4 \loch \af4 \lang1033 \langnp1033 \langfe2052 \langfenp2052 \alang1025 {\rtlch \fcs1 \af0 \afs24 \ltrch \fcs0 \fs24 \rtlch \alang1025 \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af5 \loch \f5 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe2052 \langfenp2052 \alang1025 \uc1 \u160 ?}{\rtlch \fcs1 \af0 \afs24 \ltrch \fcs0 \fs24 \rtlch \alang1025 \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af5 \loch \f5 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe2052 \langfenp2052 \alang1025 \par }\pard \plain \li480 \culi200 \ri0 \widctlpar \aspalpha \aspnum \adjustright \lin480 \